// Kryspal Media Plugin protocol v1: cast targets.
//
// Capability cast.target: Discover, Probe, Prepare, Load, Control, Status. A plugin that declares it can find playback
// devices on the local network (a DLNA television, a Chromecast), push a media URL to one and control it. The host owns
// everything that does not depend on the protocol: the device list, the cast session, the authorization behind the media URL
// and play tracking. The plugin owns the protocol and the quirks of the devices.
//
// The media URL the plugin receives is complete and carries its own authorization (a token in the path). It is limited to one
// item, can be revoked by the host at any time, and the plugin never receives a signing key or a credential. The plugin
// declares the permission network.lan (it sends multicast and connects to devices on the local network) and the manifest field
// cast_protocol ("dlna", "chromecast") so that the admin console can show what kind of devices it handles.
//
// A device is identified by Device.id, which is stable for the same physical device. Device.handle and Device.state are opaque
// to the host: the host stores them and sends them back unchanged. When a response carries updated_state, the host saves it
// first and uses it from then on (the plugin keeps what it learned about the device there, for example which variant of the
// request the device accepted). The host keeps no state of a session in the plugin: it polls Status.
//
// Timeouts: Discover and Probe 15 seconds, Load 30 seconds, Prepare, Control and Status 10 seconds.
// For capabilities the plugin does not declare, the calls return UNIMPLEMENTED.

